WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.780
The rotovap allows us to distill

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.200
at low temperatures.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.200
The low temperature is produced

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.200
because we modify the atmospheric pressure.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.200
We remove air and create a vacuum.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.600
And in this way we can promote evaporation at a very low temperature

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.800
Evaporation occurs at 50 degrees.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.800
Then we pull the volatile aromas.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.900
We produce a condensation and then the precipitation of the liquid

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:40.800
that lets us have this result and distilled water with all the aromas.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.000
Let's use a new dimension of this product. In this case, the lemon peel.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.750
So you just saw a
remarkable demonstration

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.890
from Joan Roca using this very
strange device called a rotavap.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.910
So I now want to explain
to you how a rotavap works.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.100
The rotavap-- that's a short
name of rotary evaporator--

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.680
operates by lowering the pressure
that you're cooking something in.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.300
So here again is the
phase diagram of water.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.290
And remember that we said that water can
become a gas, liquid water can become

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:15.030
a gas by increasing the temperature
from 23 degrees to 100 degrees Celsius,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.130
if you're sitting at 1
atmosphere of pressure.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.660
But you also can change the phase of
the water by changing the pressure

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.650
and keeping the temperature fixed.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.050
So if you look at the
phase diagram of water,

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:31.980
and we drop the pressure sufficiently
while maintaining the same temperature,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.760
at that point the water will become
a gas and it will actually vaporize.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.310
So the equation from this
week was that U interaction

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.960
as equal to C times k sub B
T. And what that represented,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.960
remember, is the physical balance
between the fact that on one hand,

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.370
the molecules like to stick to each
other-- that's the interaction energy--

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.850
and on the other hand, they like to
jiggle around, which is the k sub B T.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.610
And when these two are equal,
that's when the molecules start

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.364
to evaporate off, liquid becomes a gas.

00:02:00.364 --> 00:02:02.280
But now what I want you
to imagine is that you

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.990
have a liquid which is contained
of many different materials.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.729
So just for the sake of argument,
let's imagine that you have a cup

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.030
and it's filled with red
molecules and blue molecules.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.820
And imagine that the red molecules
stick to the red molecules,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.659
and they also stick
to the blue molecules,

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:18.450
and the blue molecules
stick to each other,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.158
and they also stick
to the red molecules.

00:02:20.158 --> 00:02:21.900
But they stick with different strengths.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.192
So maybe the red
molecules are very weak.

00:02:24.192 --> 00:02:27.150
That is, they bond to each other and
to the blue molecules very weakly,

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.704
whereas the blue molecules
bond more strongly.

00:02:30.704 --> 00:02:32.620
So the question that I
want you to imagine is,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.790
how can we separate the red
molecules from the blue molecules,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.480
using only the principle of the
equation that we've told you about,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.020
So let's think about this.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.640
So for the red molecules, given that
they have some interaction energy,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.480
there's some temperature at which
the red molecules will boil,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.450
if you have a substance
of pure red molecules.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.960
Similarly, if you take the blue
molecules and you put them together,

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.630
there's some temperature at which
the blue molecules will boil.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:02.274
That's the interaction energy of the
blue molecules divided by C k sub B.

00:03:02.274 --> 00:03:05.190
So what you might imagine is that
if you were sitting in a temperature

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.030
that somewhere between the red, the
temperature for the red molecules

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.120
to boil off and the blue molecules
to boil off, what you're going to get

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.490
is mainly red molecules boiling off.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.350
So this is what I want you to now do.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.790
So let's take the phase diagram that we
had with these three different phases.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.220
And I want to get rid of all
of it, except for the phase

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:27.030
boundary that corresponds to the
liquid-gas equilibrium boundary.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.980
And so this plot's the pressure
as a function of temperature.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.190
So this curve, which
basically represents

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.790
at every temperature, the pressure
at which the gas and liquid are

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.090
in equilibrium is called the vapor
pressure curve of the material.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.880
We can put the curve down
for water, and we also

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.280
add different substances on top of it.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.330
We could add alcohol, for example,
or we could add a flavor molecule.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.880
And what you see is
that these curves, which

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.060
happen at different positions
for the different materials.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.120
So for example, alcohol,
which consists of ethanol,

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.080
which consist of molecules that
are bound more weakly to each other

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.060
than water, has a different vapor
pressure than that of water.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.430
And what you see from
our curves is there

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.570
is a critical pressure where the
most volatile compounds will start

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.670
to come off much more than the others.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.240
And so the gas that's
above our substance

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.019
will be rich in the
more volatile compounds,

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.920
only by changing the pressure, not
by changing the temperature at all.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.470
As you decrease the pressure
further, more and more

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.867
of the different
compounds will come off.

00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:32.700
But if you do this
carefully and you somehow

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.340
are able to collect the volatile
compounds, the gas that you have

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.700
and reconvert it into
a liquid, then that

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.700
will allow you to separate
the two different compounds.

